::PC_U06_UA_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
For the arithmetic sequence 8, 13, 18, 23, what is the common difference
{
~B. 8#Incorrect. 8 is the first term, not the repeated change.
~C. 13#Incorrect. 13 is a term, not the common difference.
=A. 5#Correct. Each term increases by 5, so d \= 5.
~D. 10#Incorrect. The sequence increases by 5 each step, not 10.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
For a_n \= 6 + (n - 1)4, what is a_9
{
~B. 36#Incorrect. This omits part of the first-term structure.
~C. 30#Incorrect. This does not use the common difference for 8 jumps.
=A. 38#Correct. 6 + 8 times 4 \= 38.
~D. 42#Incorrect. This uses 9 jumps instead of 8. Use n - 1.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
Which explicit rule matches 20, 17, 14, 11, ...
{
=C. a_n \= 20 + (n - 1)(-3)#Correct. The first term is 20 and the common difference is -3.
~A. a_n \= 20 + 3n#Incorrect. The sequence decreases, so the common difference is not positive 3.
~B. a_n \= -3 + (n - 1)20#Incorrect. This reverses the first term and common difference.
~D. a_n \= 17 + (n - 1)(-3)#Incorrect. This starts with the second term instead of the first term.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
A pattern starts at 15 and adds 7 each step. What is the 6th term
{
~A. 42#Incorrect. This uses only part of the repeated addition.
~B. 22#Incorrect. This gives the second term only.
=D. 50#Correct. 15 + (6 - 1) times 7 \= 50.
~C. 57#Incorrect. This uses 6 jumps instead of 5.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
Why does the arithmetic explicit formula use n - 1
{
~A. The common difference is always 1#Incorrect. The common difference can be any constant.
=D. The first term has zero jumps from itself#Correct. Term 1 is already known, so term n has n - 1 changes after it.
~B. The sequence must decrease#Incorrect. Arithmetic sequences may increase or decrease.
~C. The formula is geometric#Incorrect. This question is about arithmetic explicit form.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
Which sequence is arithmetic
{
~B. 2, 5, 11, 20#Incorrect. The differences are not constant.
=A. 4, 10, 16, 22#Correct. Each term increases by 6.
~C. 1, 4, 9, 16#Incorrect. The differences are 3, 5, and 7, so they are not constant.
~D. 3, 6, 12, 24#Incorrect. This pattern multiplies by 2, so it is geometric.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.1
Question:
For 30, 25, 20, 15, what is d
{
~A. 5#Incorrect. The sequence decreases, so the difference is negative.
~B. 30#Incorrect. 30 is the first term, not d.
=C. -5#Correct. Each term decreases by 5, so d \= -5.
~D. -10#Incorrect. The repeated change is -5, not -10.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
For the geometric sequence 2, 10, 50, 250, what is the common ratio
{
~A. 10#Incorrect. 10 is a term, not the repeated multiplier.
~B. 2#Incorrect. 2 is the first term, not the common ratio.
~D. 8#Incorrect. 8 is a difference, not the ratio.
=C. 5#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 5.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q09::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
For a_n \= 3(2)², what is a_6
{
~A. 192#Incorrect. This uses 2⁶ instead of 2⁵.
~B. 36#Incorrect. This treats the pattern like repeated addition.
=C. 96#Correct. 3 times 2⁵ \= 32, so 3 times 2⁵ \= 32, so 3 times 2⁵ \= 96.
~D. 64#Incorrect. This omits the factor 3.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q10::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
Which rule matches 81, 27, 9, 3, ...
{
=D. a_n \= 81(1/3)²#Correct. The first term is 81 and the ratio is 1/3.
~A. a_n \= 81 + (n - 1)(-54)#Incorrect. This arithmetic rule does not match all terms.
~B. a_n \= 27(1/3)²#Incorrect. This starts with the second term.
~C. a_n \= 81(3)²#Incorrect. Multiplying by 3 would increase the terms.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q11::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
A value starts at 12 and triples each step. What is the 5th term
{
~A. 2916#Incorrect. This uses five multiplications instead of four.
~C. 60#Incorrect. This treats tripling like adding 12.
~D. 36#Incorrect. This is the second term only.
=B. 972#Correct. 12 times 3⁴ \= 972.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q12::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
How do you test whether a sequence is geometric
{
~A. Add all terms#Incorrect. Adding terms gives a sum, not the sequence type.
=B. Divide consecutive terms and check for the same ratio#Correct. A geometric sequence has a constant ratio.
~C. Look only at a_1#Incorrect. One term cannot identify a pattern.
~D. Subtract consecutive terms and check for the same difference#Incorrect. Subtraction tests arithmetic sequences.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q13::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
Which sequence is geometric
{
~A. 6, 10, 14, 18#Incorrect. This sequence has constant difference 4, so it is arithmetic.
~B. 1, 4, 9, 16#Incorrect. This is a square-number pattern, not a constant ratio.
=C. 5, 15, 45, 135#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 3.
~D. 2, 5, 9, 14#Incorrect. The differences change and the ratios are not constant.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q14::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.2
Question:
For 64, 32, 16, 8, what is r
{
=D. 1/2#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 1/2.
~A. -2#Incorrect. The terms stay positive and halve, so r is not -2.
~B. 32#Incorrect. 32 is a term, not the ratio.
~C. 2#Incorrect. Multiplying by 2 would increase the terms.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q15::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
What is an arithmetic series
{
~B. A list with a constant ratio#Incorrect. Constant ratio describes geometric sequences.
~C. A single arithmetic term#Incorrect. A series is a sum of terms, not one term.
~D. A difference quotient#Incorrect. Difference quotients measure function change.
=A. The sum of terms from an arithmetic sequence#Correct. A series is a sum, and arithmetic identifies the sequence type.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q16::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Find 4 + 8 + 12 + 16 + 20.
{
~A. 80#Incorrect. This overcounts the terms.
=B. 60#Correct. The sum is 60.
~C. 20#Incorrect. 20 is the last term only.
~D. 24#Incorrect. 24 is only first plus last, not the whole sum.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q17::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Which formula gives a finite arithmetic series sum
{
=B. S_n \= n(a_1 + a_n) / 2#Correct. This is the finite arithmetic series formula.
~A. a_n \= a_1 + (n - 1)d#Incorrect. This finds one arithmetic sequence term.
~C. a_n \= a_1 r²#Incorrect. This is a geometric sequence formula.
~D. S \= a_1 / (1 - r)#Incorrect. This is an infinite geometric series formula.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q18::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
For 5, 9, 13, ..., what is the 10th term needed to find S_10
{
~A. 90#Incorrect. This is not the 10th term.
~C. 230#Incorrect. This is the sum, not the last term.
~D. 45#Incorrect. This uses 10 jumps instead of 9.
=B. 41#Correct. 5 + 9 times 4 \= 41.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q19::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Why does S_n \= n(a_1 + a_n) / 2 divide by 2
{
=A. It uses the average of the first and last terms#Correct. The formula uses number of terms times the average term value.
~B. Every series has two terms#Incorrect. A finite series can have many terms.
~C. The common difference must be halved#Incorrect. The formula averages term values, not the common difference.
~D. It changes the series to geometric#Incorrect. Dividing by 2 does not change the series type.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q20::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
A runner goes 3 miles on day 1 and adds 2 miles each day for 6 days. What total is represented
{
~B. 18 miles#Incorrect. This adds the starting amount six times and misses the increase.
~C. 36 miles#Incorrect. This does not include all terms correctly.
~D. 13 miles#Incorrect. 13 is only the last term.
=A. 48 miles#Correct. The terms are 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 and the sum is 48.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q21::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
If a problem asks for a total over several arithmetic terms, what should you use
{
~B. Only the common difference#Incorrect. The common difference helps, but it is not the total.
=A. Arithmetic series reasoning#Correct. A total over terms is a series question.
~C. Geometric ratio only#Incorrect. A ratio applies to geometric patterns.
~D. Difference quotient only#Incorrect. Difference quotients apply to function change.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q22::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
What is a geometric series
{
=C. The sum of terms from a geometric sequence#Correct. A geometric series adds terms from a geometric sequence.
~A. One term from a sequence#Incorrect. A series is a sum, not one term.
~B. A rate of change#Incorrect. Rate of change is a function concept.
~D. A list with constant difference#Incorrect. Constant difference describes arithmetic sequences.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q23::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Find the sum of the first 4 terms of 3, 6, 12, 24, ...
{
~A. 24#Incorrect. 24 is only the fourth term.
=D. 45#Correct. 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 \= 45.
~B. 48#Incorrect. This doubles the last term rather than summing terms.
~C. 90#Incorrect. This overcounts the sum.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q24::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
When does an infinite geometric series have a finite sum
{
=D. When |r| is less than 1#Correct. The terms shrink toward 0 when |r| < 1.
~A. When r equals 1#Incorrect. If r \= 1, terms do not shrink.
~B. Always#Incorrect. Not every infinite geometric series converges.
~C. When r is greater than 1#Incorrect. If r > 1, terms grow.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q25::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Find the infinite sum of 10 + 5 + 2.5 + ...
{
~B. 17.5#Incorrect. This only adds visible terms.
~C. 10#Incorrect. This is the first term only.
~D. No finite sum#Incorrect. Since |r| < 1, the sum is finite.
=A. 20#Correct. S \= 10 / (1 - 1/2) \= 20.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q26::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Why does 4 + 8 + 16 + ... not have a finite infinite sum
{
=A. The common ratio is 2, so the terms grow#Correct. Since |2| is not less than 1, it does not converge.
~B. There are only three terms#Incorrect. The dots show the series continues forever.
~C. The ratio is 1/2#Incorrect. The ratio is 2, not 1/2.
~D. The first term is even#Incorrect. Being even does not determine convergence.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q27::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Which formula is for a convergent infinite geometric series
{
~A. S_n \= n(a_1 + a_n) / 2#Incorrect. This is the arithmetic series formula.
~B. a_n \= a_1 + (n - 1)d#Incorrect. This is an arithmetic sequence formula.
=C. S \= a_1 / (1 - r)#Correct. This formula applies when |r| < 1.
~D. f(b)-f(a)#Incorrect. This is part of rate-of-change work, not a series formula.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q28::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
For 6 + 3 + 1.5 + ..., what is r
{
~A. 3#Incorrect. 3 is the second term, not the ratio.
=D. 1/2#Correct. Each term is half the previous term.
~B. 6#Incorrect. 6 is the first term.
~C. 2#Incorrect. Multiplying by 2 would increase terms.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q29::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.5
Question:
What must a recursive rule include
{
~A. Only the common difference#Incorrect. A difference alone does not tell where to begin.
=D. An initial term and a rule for the next term#Correct. A recursive rule needs a start and a way to continue.
~B. Only the final answer#Incorrect. A final answer is not a rule.
~C. Only a graph#Incorrect. A graph is not the required notation.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q30::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.5
Question:
Which rule is recursive for 7, 11, 15, 19, ...
{
~B. a_n \= 7(4)²#Incorrect. This is geometric and does not match the pattern.
~C. a_1 \= 4; a_n \= a_(n-1) + 7#Incorrect. This reverses the first term and change.
~D. a_n \= 7 + (n - 1)4#Incorrect. This is explicit, not recursive.
=A. a_1 \= 7; a_n \= a_(n-1) + 4#Correct. It gives the first term and uses the previous term plus 4.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q31::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.5
Question:
Which rule finds the 50th term directly
{
~A. Recursive rule#Incorrect. Recursive rules require building from previous terms.
=C. Explicit rule#Correct. Explicit rules use n to find a term directly.
~B. Initial term only#Incorrect. An initial term alone is not enough.
~D. Common ratio only#Incorrect. A ratio alone is not a complete rule.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q32::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.5
Question:
Given a_1 \= 3 and a_n \= 2a_(n-1), what is a_4
{
~A. 12#Incorrect. 12 is a_3, not a_4.
~B. 18#Incorrect. This treats the pattern like adding 5.
~D. 6#Incorrect. 6 is a_2, not a_4.
=C. 24#Correct. The terms are 3, 6, 12, 24.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q33::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.5
Question:
Which statement correctly compares explicit and recursive notation
{
~A. Explicit always adds; recursive always multiplies#Incorrect. Both notation types can describe different pattern types.
=C. Explicit finds directly; recursive uses previous terms#Correct. This is the key difference.
~B. Recursive rules need no starting value#Incorrect. Recursive rules need an initial term.
~D. Both only work for arithmetic sequences#Incorrect. Both can represent arithmetic or geometric patterns.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q34::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.5
Question:
Why is a_1 necessary in a recursive rule
{
~A. It replaces the common ratio#Incorrect. It does not replace the ratio or difference.
~B. It makes the rule explicit#Incorrect. Recursive and explicit rules are different forms.
~C. It gives the final term#Incorrect. a_1 is the first term, not the final term.
=D. It tells where the sequence starts#Correct. Without the initial term, the recursion has no starting point.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q35::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
For f(x)\=x², what is the average rate of change from x\=1 to x\=4
{
~A. 3#Incorrect. 3 is only the input change.
~C. 15#Incorrect. 15 is the output change before division.
=B. 5#Correct. (16 - 1) / (4 - 1) \= 5.
~D. 6#Incorrect. This does not use output change divided by input change.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q36::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
What does average rate of change compare
{
~A. Only the final output#Incorrect. A rate uses two output values.
=B. Output change to input change#Correct. It is change in output divided by change in input.
~C. Only the first input#Incorrect. A rate uses an interval.
~D. Input change to output change#Incorrect. This reverses the ratio.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q37::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
For f(x)\=3x+1, what is the simplified difference quotient
{
~A. 3x+1#Incorrect. This is the function, not the difference quotient.
~B. h#Incorrect. h cancels after simplification.
=C. 3#Correct. The numerator simplifies to 3h, and 3h/h \= 3.
~D. x+3#Incorrect. This combines terms incorrectly.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q38::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
In (f(x+h)-f(x))/h, what does h represent
{
~A. The first term of a sequence#Incorrect. This notation is about function change, not sequence terms.
~B. The common ratio#Incorrect. Common ratio belongs to geometric sequences.
=D. The input change#Correct. h is the change from x to x+h.
~C. The output value#Incorrect. h is not an output value.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q39::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
If outputs approach 8 as x approaches 3 from both sides, what is the informal limit statement
{
=A. f(x) approaches 8 as x approaches 3#Correct. This accurately describes the approaching behavior.
~B. f(x) equals 3 for all x#Incorrect. Approaching does not mean every output equals 3.
~C. x approaches 8 as f(x) approaches 3#Incorrect. This reverses input and output roles.
~D. There is no possible statement#Incorrect. The table behavior supports a clear informal statement.
}

::PC_U06_UA_Q40::
Question ID: PC_U06_UA_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
For f(x)\=2x+5, what is the average rate of change from x\=1 to x\=6
{
=B. 2#Correct. A linear function with slope 2 has average rate of change 2.
~A. 12#Incorrect. This is an output value, not the rate.
~C. 17#Incorrect. This is an output value, not the rate.
~D. 5#Incorrect. 5 is the input change, not the rate.\}
